name: tls and irc
boss: Rebase
flavor: Your history is mine to rewrite.
syntax: -
brief: Everything nc carries is plaintext -- readable and editable by anyone on
brief: the path. tlstunnel is this system's answer: it wraps a plain connection
brief: in TLS, checking the server's certificate against trust anchors compiled
brief: into the binary itself, not read from a file some attacker could edit.
brief:
brief: ii is irc as a directory tree. Each channel is a folder with an `out`
brief: file you read and an `in` fifo you write to. No client, no ui -- which
brief: means tail, grep and echo are the client.
---
q: {open an encrypted connection to @host@:443 to pipe commands through|wrap a session to @host@:443 in tls, ready to drive by hand|reach @host@ on port 443 over tls, ready to speak the protocol}
a: tlstunnel - @host@ 443
why: live-net
ref: tlstunnel
teach: tlstunnel
hint: tlstunnel takes - for stdin/stdout, then the host and port to wrap
---
q: {fetch @host@'s page over https by hand, through a tls tunnel|speak http to @host@ by hand, through a tls tunnel|hand-craft an http request to @host@, tunnel it through tls}
a: printf 'HEAD / HTTP/1.0\r\nHost: @host@\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n' | tlstunnel - @host@ 443
why: live-net
ref: tlstunnel
teach: tlstunnel
hint: pipe a raw http request into tlstunnel the same way you would into nc
---
q: %count% "Internet Security Research Group" hits in /bin/tlstunnel
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep -c 'Internet Security Research Group'
cmp: ref
ref: strings
teach: strings
hint: they are in the binary, not in a file -- there is nothing on disk to edit
